{"name":"Soap Bubble","hex":"#b2dcef","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"soap bubble","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A very thin film of soapy water that forms a sphere with an iridescent surface.","Anything that is attractive but insubstantial."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/soap_bubble"}],"entries":[{"title":"Soap bubble","blocks":[{"text":"A soap bubble is an extremely thin film of soap or detergent and water enclosing air that forms a hollow sphere with an iridescent surface. Soap bubbles usually last for only a few seconds before bursting, either on their own or on contact with another object. They are often used for children's enjoyment, but they are also used in artistic performances. Assembling many bubbles results in foam."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Reflection_in_a_soap_bubble_edit.jpg/330px-Reflection_in_a_soap_bubble_edit.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soap_bubble"},{"title":"Soap","blocks":[{"text":"Soap is a salt of a fatty acid used for cleaning and lubricating products as well as other applications. In a domestic setting, soaps, specifically \"toilet soaps\", are surfactants typically used for washing, bathing, and other types of housekeeping. In industrial settings, soaps are used as thickeners, components of some lubricants, emulsifiers, and catalysts."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/Handmade_soap_cropped_and_simplified.jpg/330px-Handmade_soap_cropped_and_simplified.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soap"},{"title":"Lavender (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Lavender is a light shade of purple or violet. It applies particularly to the color of the flower of the same name."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/Lavandula_fields.jpg/330px-Lavandula_fields.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lavender_(color)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/Soap_Film.jpg/500px-Soap_Film.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/Soap_Film.jpg/1280px-Soap_Film.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/Soap_Film.jpg/500px-Soap_Film.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Close-up of soap film, which makes up a soap bubble","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASoap_Film.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/Soapbubbles1b.jpg/500px-Soapbubbles1b.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/Soapbubbles1b.jpg/500px-Soapbubbles1b.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Many bubbles make foam","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASoapbubbles1b.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/Bubble_on_an_ultrahydrophobic_surface.jpg/500px-Bubble_on_an_ultrahydrophobic_surface.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7c/Bubble_on_an_ultrahydrophobic_surface.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/Bubble_on_an_ultrahydrophobic_surface.jpg/500px-Bubble_on_an_ultrahydrophobic_surface.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABubble_on_an_ultrahydrophobic_surface.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.w